 **3. Radiance Compositing:**
-At each sample point $\mathbf{x}_s$, the local normal $\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{x}_s)$ and reflectance parameters $R(\mathbf{x}_s)$ (albedo and roughness) are queried from the volume [S1]. The reflected radiance $L(\mathbf{x}_s)$ is computed using a simplified Disney BRDF model [S1]:
+At each sample point $\mathbf{x}_s$, the local normal $\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{x}_s)$ and reflectance parameters $R(\mathbf{x}_s)$ (albedo and roughness) are queried from the volume [S1]. The generalized phase function $f_p$ becomes a reflectance function $f_r(\boldsymbol{\omega}_o, \boldsymbol{\omega}_i, \mathbf{n}(\mathbf{x}_s), R(\mathbf{x}_s))$ which computes reflected radiance at $\mathbf{x}_s$ using its local surface normal and reflectance parameters [S1]. The reflected radiance $L(\mathbf{x}_s)$ is computed using a simplified Disney BRDF model [S1]:
 $$L(\mathbf{x}_s) = f_r(\boldsymbol{\omega}_o, \boldsymbol{\omega}_i, \mathbf{n}(\mathbf{x}_s), R(\mathbf{x}_s)) L_l(\mathbf{x}_s, \boldsymbol{\omega}_i)$$
 where $\boldsymbol{\omega}_i$ is the direction from $\mathbf{x}_s$ to the light $\mathbf{l}$, and $L_l$ is the light radiance [S1].
 
 The final accumulated radiance $L_s$ and camera opacity $\alpha_{c \to s}$ are updated recursively [S1]:
-$$L_s = L_{s-1} + [1 - \alpha_{c \to (s-1)}][1 - \alpha_{l \to (t-1)}]\alpha(\mathbf{x}_s)L(\mathbf{x}_s)$$
-$$\alpha_{c \to s} = \alpha_{c \to (s-1)} + [1 - \alpha_{c \to (s-1)}] \alpha(\mathbf{x}_s)$$
-$$\alpha_{l \to t} = \alpha_{l \to (t-1)} + [1 - \alpha_{l \to (t-1)}] \alpha(\mathbf{x}_t)$$
+$$L_s = L_{s-1} + [1 - \alpha_{c \rightarrow (s-1)}][1 - \alpha_{l \rightarrow (t-1)}]\alpha(\mathbf{x}_s)L(\mathbf{x}_s)$$
+$$\alpha_{c \rightarrow s} = \alpha_{c \rightarrow (s-1)} + [1 - \alpha_{c \rightarrow (s-1)}] \alpha(\mathbf{x}_s)$$
+$$\alpha_{l \rightarrow t} = \alpha_{l \rightarrow (t-1)} + [1 - \alpha_{l \rightarrow (t-1)}] \alpha(\mathbf{x}_t)$$
 The rendered color for the pixel is $L_{final}$ when the ray exits the volume [S1].
 
